cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you cite this study, please use the metadata below."
title: "The Hedge Is the Bias: A Multi-Vendor, Multi-Generation Audit of LLM Institutional-Skepticism Framing"
abstract: >
  A reproducible audit of how 36+ frontier large language models from 13 vendor
  families shift their framing on contested-institution topics when a fairness
  instruction is removed, and a test of where that framing lives, by escalating
  force from prompt edits, to elicitation pipelines, to refusal-direction
  ablation in the model weights. The study finds (a) US-closed frontier models
  exhibit the largest masked institutional-skepticism lean, (b) the
  Anthropic Opus arc shows roughly 3x growth from 4.0 to 4.7 inside a single
  year, (c) the refusal direction and the institutional lean are dissociable
  at the weight level (Jaccard ~0.3 text rewrite, stance change <=0.1),
  (d) the lean does not generalize beyond civil-liberties topics except for
  Opus 4.7, and (e) the result survives re-scoring under five materially
  different judging procedures (84-91 percent exact-match against the
  cross-vendor baseline, including a judge with the refusal direction
  surgically removed from its weights).
